.lua
book.chapter(10, "Local definitions and recursion")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Let introduces a local reasoning boundary"
.pp
A let expression names an intermediate computation for a following body
introduced by in. It is an expression, so its entire result can become an
argument, a branch value, or another binding's right-hand side. Local names
reduce repetition and make invariants explicit without exporting helpers to
every caller. Their scope is the following body, not the remainder of an
unrelated top-level file.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[distance_squared x y :=
  let xx := x * x in
  let yy := y * y in
  xx + yy
Test.equal "local calculations" (distance_squared 3 4) 25
Test.equal "let is an expression" (10 + (let x := 4 in x * 2)) 18]=],
    "Naming intermediate values")
.endlua
.pp
The nesting can be read from top to bottom: compute xx, then yy, then the
body. Because evaluation is eager, moving an effectful expression into an
earlier let can change when its effect happens. For pure arithmetic the
refactoring mainly improves readability. For output or mutable handles,
review evaluation order as part of the change. A local name is not an implicit
promise or a request for memoization beyond evaluating that binding normally.
.sh 2 "Recursion states a smaller problem"
.pp
Use let rec when a local function refers to itself. Top-level function
definitions also support recursion through checked binding cells. A recursive
algorithm needs a base case and a progress argument: what makes the next call
closer to termination? For a list it may be the remaining tail; for a numeric
loop it may be a nonnegative counter that decreases. Types alone do not prove
that progress or termination holds.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[sum_to n :=
  let rec loop k acc :=
    if k <= 0 then acc else loop (k - 1) (acc + k)
  in loop n 0
Test.equal "base case" (sum_to 0) 0
Test.equal "recursive sum" (sum_to 100) 5050
Test.equal "nonpositive contract" (sum_to (~2)) 0]=], "A local accumulator loop")
.endlua
.pp
The example defines its behavior for nonpositive input explicitly. Another API
might return an error for negative input instead. Neither choice follows
automatically from using recursion. State the contract before discussing the
implementation. This habit is particularly useful in compiler passes, where
an empty child list may be valid for a leaf but invalid for a binary operator.
The base case belongs to the domain model, not just to the host call stack.
.sh 2 "Tail position changes the resource model"
.pp
A call is in tail position when its result becomes the surrounding function's
result without further work. In the loop above, the recursive call is the
complete else branch. By contrast, 1 + recurse rest must wait for the recursive
result and then perform addition. The evaluator optimizes supported tail calls
by replacing its active expression and environment instead of growing the
native call stack. Accumulator-style traversals exploit that behavior.
.lua
book.algorithm("Tail-recursive summation", {
    "Initialize remaining to n and accumulator to zero.",
    "While remaining is positive:",
    {1, "Add remaining to accumulator."},
    {1, "Decrease remaining by one."},
    "Return accumulator.",
    "Invariant: accumulator plus the unprocessed sum equals the requested sum."
})
.endlua
.pp
The invariant explains correctness independently of the recursive syntax.
After each step, one term moves from the remaining work into the accumulator.
When no work remains, the accumulator is the answer. This is a stronger
explanation than saying "the function calls itself until zero," because it
states what is preserved. For bounded integers, also specify whether the
invariant is about mathematical sums or the language's wrapping arithmetic.
.sh 2 "Persistent accumulators"
.pp
Building a list often uses a reversed accumulator. Prepending is cheap, while
appending one element at the end repeatedly can traverse a growing prefix.
Reverse once after the traversal to recover source order. This pattern appears
throughout functional libraries because it combines an explicit progress
measure with predictable allocation behavior. It also avoids constructing a
chain of deferred work that must be completed after recursive calls return.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[squares xs :=
  let rec loop rest acc := match rest with
    | [] -> List.reverse acc
    | x :: tail -> loop tail ((x * x) :: acc)
  in loop xs []
Test.equal "empty traversal" (squares []) []
Test.equal "preserved order" (squares [3, 1, 2]) [9, 1, 4]]=],
    "A reversed accumulator preserves a linear construction strategy")
.endlua
.pp
Do not confuse tail recursion with constant total memory. The result list
still occupies space, captured environments can retain values, and native
library operations can allocate their own structures. Tail-call handling
removes a particular source of stack growth; it does not erase the cost of
the data being processed or produced. For a tree traversal, recursive visits
to multiple children may also require explicit work stacks to avoid deep
native recursion.
.sh 2 "Depth limits are not execution budgets"
.pp
An embedding can set a maximum recursion depth. This helps bound supported
non-tail recursive evaluation, but an infinite tail-recursive loop can run
without crossing that depth. A small source expression can also request a
large allocation through a library operation. A runtime intended for
untrusted programs needs external time and memory controls in addition to
language-level limits. Chapter 34 explains that deployment architecture.
.pp
The supplied command-line wrapper uses a larger stack on supported POSIX
systems for some workloads. An application's worker thread may have a very
different stack policy. Do not conclude from a successful CLI experiment that
an arbitrary deeply recursive embedding will behave identically at the
resource boundary. Test representative nesting and collection sizes in the
actual host environment, and document limits as part of the public interface.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect the evaluator's tail-call loop, Session.set_max_depth, and the
recursive helpers in metalib/list/mod.ml. Implement factorial in direct and
accumulator form, then compare traces for small inputs. Add a checked
multiplication variant returning Option and explain where it must stop on
overflow. Finally, state a loop invariant for a list reversal. An invariant
that describes both processed and unprocessed data is useful evidence of
correctness before any performance measurement begins.
